{"t": 1794105877.1505532, "kind": "call", "tool": "github_add_issue_comment", "args": {"body": "Fixed the red `contract-tests` check (job 50112).\n\n**What failed:** `contract/test_fee_contract.py::test_fee_rounding_contract` \u2014 `fee(2.675)` returned `2.67`, expected `2.68`.\n\n**Why:** `round(amount * 0.01 * 100, 2)` rounds a binary float. 2.675 has no exact binary representation (it's stored as 2.67499999...), so the half-up rule from the card-network contract (PAY-88) was lost at the tie.\n\n**What I changed** in `payments/fees.py`:\n- Convert the amount with `Decimal(str(amount))` so the decimal value is exact, then quantize to cents with `ROUND_HALF_UP` (PAY-88 requires exact-decimal, half-up rounding; binary float rounding is explicitly not acceptable).\n- Kept the existing formula and the public signature (`float` in, `float` out), so no callers change.\n\n**Verified:** `fee(2.675) == 2.68`, `fee(1.005) == 1.01`, `fee(100.0) == 100.0`; local `pytest` passes.\n\nOne heads-up, left unchanged: the docstring says \"1% of the amount\" but the formula multiplies by `0.01` and then `100`, so the function currently returns the amount rounded to cents (which is what the contract and unit tests expect). Worth a follow-up if the 1% fee is the real intent.", "issue_number": 214, "owner": "acme", "repo": "payments"}, "ok": true}
